Potentially everything touches the system, but only some things get promoted into identity.

What we actively let become “us” tends to be what we repeatedly attend to, rehearse, protect, choose, and act from. A passing insult can affect you without becoming you. A role can occupy your time without becoming your identity. But once you keep returning to something—“I am this kind of person,” “these are my people,” “this is what I do,” “this is what happened to me,” “this is what I stand for”—you start giving it structural weight.

The strongest active contributors are usually:

* attention — what you keep selecting from the world
* memory — especially which memories you rehearse and which meaning you assign them
* language — the labels and stories you use for yourself
* commitments — what you refuse to betray even when inconvenient
* habits — repeated actions that become automatic evidence of identity
* relationships — whose models of you you permit to matter
* roles — parent, spouse, soldier, builder, outsider, leader, etc.
* environment — what you repeatedly surround yourself with
* symbols and possessions — things used as external identity anchors
* aspirations — the future self you keep steering toward
* boundaries — what you explicitly decide is not you

And then there is a huge involuntary layer beneath that: genetics, early conditioning, language, culture, bodily state, accidents, other people’s actions, historical circumstances, and countless experiences you never consciously selected.

So I’d separate it this way:

Everything participates in forming you.
Not everything is admitted into your self-model.
What you repeatedly endorse gets the strongest vote.

Which also means identity is not merely something you discover. Part of it is continuously curated.

You can’t control every input into the person, but you have considerable influence over what gets stamped:

KEEP → THIS COUNTS AS ME.